A||||28||grinthock||Rich Running - Continued - Expert Help Please?||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||11-14-2001||07:57 PM||||Ok everyone here goes.<P>Still working on the rich issue.<P>2 issues, 1 the car has LOTS of power, but runs in a rich condition 100% of the time (have scantool)<P>the second problem is, at a light, the car wants to DIE, it idles down, lower and lower until the oil pressure sensor kills the engine. THIS problem is intermittant.<P><BR>When COLD the car runs fine (still rich mind you, that's normal we know this)  but when an open loop is acheived it starts this nonsense.<P>Replaced the IAC, no dice.<P><BR>Here are some scantool #'s<P>in a closed loop condition<P>at idle.<P>the car cross's from lean to rich<BR>O2 voltage .2 to .7<BR>MAT is 17 degrees<BR>map sensor at 1.98 v<BR>IAC 72 steps<BR>rpm 925<BR>Integrator 133<BR>BLM 120-130<BR>spark adv 18 deg<BR>pulse width 2.5<BR>EGR Diag OPEN<P><BR>Any ideas?  <P>I am thinking that the EGR solonoid is keeping the EGR open or something, this is a possible?  I just recently replaced the EGR valve, checked the tube and cleaned it.<P>HELP!  Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroworld.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroworld.com</A>  The place to go for Fiero INFO! <BR>Home of my 87 GT and 85 GT<BR>Justin C.<BR>Engineer, Designer and<BR>Technical Trainer for Nortel Networks Business Products.||||reg||1
Z||000001||grinthock||11-14-2001||08:05 PM||||Thinking again.<P>The EGR Solonoid could be ONE of my problems, maybe I have 2, one causing a rich condition and one causing the stalling.<P>Next time the car decides to sputter at a light I will pull over and jump out and try pulling the vac line.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tina||11-14-2001||08:37 PM||||Well, asked my Engine Guru. <BR>His response,(grumbel, grumbel)<P>With a MAP sensor voltage of 2volts, it appears, that there is a low manifold vacuum<BR>causing a rich condition.<P>Need to find the vacuum leak, could be gaskets,EGR, or poor intake valve seating.<P>There could be other reason for low manifold vacuum, these are just the 3 most common problems.<P>(grumbel, grumbel)||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||11-14-2001||08:43 PM||||IAC & injector pulse width both look high for 925 RPMs.  Is this a smooth idle or hunting idle, what was the ECM coolant temp reading.  Also, there is NO oil pressure cut-out.  Either your eng RPM got so low the engine couldn't stay running or your fuel pump relay is gone and when the low pressure came on, no fuel.  The low pressure won't override the FP relay.  Also, w/ regard to what Tina said, get a vacuum gauge and check the manifold vacuum as you could also have a bad MAP sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||grinthock||11-14-2001||10:41 PM||||If I had a bad MAP Sensor wouldn't I be throwing a code?  Darn ECM, wish it was a tad more intelligent!<P>Thanks for the help so far everyone.<P>I managed to check hte EGR, it was stalling out tonight when I went out, so I pulled the egr Vac line and plugged it for a sec, nothing changed meaning it's not getting stuck open.<P>And my idle is hunting.<P>What I can't seem to find is a good VAC Line guide that shows where all the lines go to and from.<P>Question....  How can I test the lines for vac?  Also this low vac condition what tool do I need to test.<P>Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||11-14-2001||10:58 PM||||Pull the vacuum line that goes to the brake booster from the manifold. plug it at the manifold and try it. this is a great place for a vacuum leak and nobody notices it because it is pretty well hidden. If it is pre 88 then it has a filter on the firewall that is plastic and it breaks from the heat. If it is bad just splice the hose together with a connector as it is not really needed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||11-14-2001||11:31 PM||||I picked up a MAP from a wrecker today, I will install and try it tommorow to see if it makes a diff||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tina||11-15-2001||10:14 PM||||[QUOTE]<BR><B>What I can't seem to find is a good VAC Line guide that shows where all the lines go to and from.</B>[QUOTE]<P>Look under the decklid, hopefully the sticker<BR>is still there.<P>[quote]<B> Also this low vac condition what tool do I need to test.</B>[quote]<P>vacuum gauge... <BR>Cheapest way to find <B>external</B> leak is still the gardenhose,.....point and listen until you hear the sound a kid makes trying to get the last of the pop with the straw out of a cup.<BR><B>Internal</B> leak ( gas, exhaust or crank case ventilation)<BR>Easly seen with Four gas analyzer,but that's <BR>a shop test.<BR> <BR>Tina<BR>BTW Next time it act's up, try this...<BR>Pull the vaccuum line that connects the fuel pressure regulatur and Map sensor to manifold vacuum. <BR>Look for fuel in the vaccumline.<BR>(Just a little wetness is too much)<BR>That would cause a rich condition, stalling at idle, low manifold vacuum readings and other undesirable affects.<BR>Just an Idea.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
