Z||000025||Krashdavus||11-29-2001||02:55 PM||||Replace the O2 sensor, they are cheap.  If the "non-sense" starts in closed loop, that's when the ECM starts using the O2 data to set fuel trim, in conjunction with MAP (engine load) and IAT & ECT.  You can check the fuel pressure with a fuel pressue gauge kit.  There is a Schraeder valve on the fuel rail on the right hand side.  If you have access to one, just screw it on an check pressue.  I think spec is 38-44 psi.<BR>If you had a fuel pressure regulator leak, there would be fuel in the vaccuum hoses, and the loss of pressure would cause a stall.  The extra fuel being sucked in through vaccuum hoses would foul the plugs.  Second, EGR leaks at idle cause lean stalls, not rich stalls.  An egr leak would cause a rough running engine/stall depending on the severity of the leak.  It would happen cold or warm.  So go with the O2.  A good O2 will toggle from 0.050 mV to .999 mV.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Krashdavus||11-29-2001||02:59 PM||||Replace the 02 sensor.  If the problem is closed loop specfic, first place to look is the O2.  In open loop, the ECM uses data from ECT, IAT, and MAP (also MAF if your vehicle is equipped).  Once in closed loop, the data gets fine tuned and the ECM accesses more fuel cell calibrations.<P>A leaking FPR (broken diaphram) will cause vaccuum hoses to be soaked with fuel, and should foul your spark plugs.<P>EGR leaks at idle cause a lean condition, not a rich condition.  This would be noticed both cold and hot.<P>Go with the O2 first, then come back.||||reg||1
Z||000027||grinthock||11-30-2001||12:12 AM||||Ok I think the story I am getting is REPLACE O2 now!  Go figure.<P>Any comments on this....<P>The problem is flakey, doesn't do it all the time, eg.  On the way home tonight it ran PERFECT and ran cooler, this happens alot, the problem goes away for a day then comes bakc<P>Still think it's O2?||||reg||1
Z||000028||Krashdavus||11-30-2001||04:26 PM||||Intermittent=Wiring.<P>You could have a wire grounding out, or chafing, or even a terminal that's spread just a tad.  Check that O2 and it's connector.  Good luck, these are even tougher to find, let alone compounded by a tight engine bay.  Sucks to be you rigth now.  Then again, your probably a Tiger cat fan...lol.||||reg||1
